export const meta: ResearchMeta = {
  title: 'End2End Hackerman',
  dek: 'I stumbled across an interesting topic that I thought would be worth sharing and dissecting, both for myself and for you.',
  date: '2026-09-10',
  slug: 'end2end-hackerman',
  stats: [],
  tags: ['Quantum Computing', 'Cryptography'],
};
---

<BaseLayout title={meta.title} description={meta.dek}>
  <article class="note">
    <a class="back" href={withBase('/scratchnotes')}>&larr; Scratchnotes</a>
    <p class="date">{meta.date}</p>
    <h1>{meta.title}</h1>

    <p>
      I stumbled across an interesting topic that I thought would be worth
      sharing and dissecting, both for myself and for you. It fits neatly
      into my website's premise of exploring topics to understand where we
      are headed as well as something that needs a deeper dive to satiate
      my curiosity. I wanted to discuss Quantum Computing, and the fresh
      discussion surrounding it by industrial leaders, and across fintech.
      It was one of the three themes of this year's GFF, along with Agentic
      AI and Tokenization. The reason that Quantum Computing specifically
      peaked my interests was because it hasn't arrived, it's going to take
      some amount of time to crack it, but the discussions on how to rein
      it in and balance its potential from a security perspective is
      already being spoken about extensively. Before getting into what I
      mean, I wanted to make explicit that I will be learning and exploring
      this side of tech along with writing it out. I came across this topic
      when I went to one of my pre-planned lectures at GFF earlier to
      ensure I got a seat. A session on safety surrounding Quantum
      computing was going on, and I ended up listening in, googling stuff
      while the panelists were discussing QKDs. I decided to then attend an
      additional workshop on firms being crypto agile in the world of
      quantum computers, which was again, a very fascinating eye opener of
      where we are headed and how much uncertainty, without inside access,
      you'd be blinded too. The reason that I want to write this and
      hopefully continue to democratize information in this space.
    </p>

    <p>
      It's first important to understand why and where the discussion of
      quantum computing has come from, and why it's a topic of concern. So,
      the idea of quantum computing has been around for a while, in simple
      terms it is an advanced machine that uses the mechanics of quantum
      physics to solve certain problems in fundamentally different ways
      from classical computers, with the potential for major speedups on
      specific tasks. When I read this, the first thing that popped up was,
      what does it mean to leverage the mechanics of quantum physics, very
      abstract phrasing of capability. So I did a little bit of a deeper
      dive into what leveraging the ideas of quantum mechanics mean, in my
      best attempt at keeping it simple and digestible:
    </p>

    <p>
      The first basic idea is that quantum computers differ in how they use
      the rules of physics, particularly the strange behaviour that emerges
      at very small, quantum scales. What we know is that a standard
      computer works with predictable binary states, like a switch being
      either on or off, represented as 0s and 1s. Quantum computers, on the
      other hand, leverage the fact that quantum systems can behave in ways
      that are fundamentally different from these classical states,
      including having wave-like properties. So, when they say that quantum
      computers leverage this nature of quantum particles, there are three
      key concepts that help explain how they process information:
    </p>

    <div class="concept">
      <h3>1. Superposition (The Spinning Coin)</h3>
      <p>
        In classical physics, an object can only be in one place or state
        at a time. In quantum mechanics, a particle can exist in multiple
        possibilities simultaneously.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Rough Analogy:</strong> Think of a flipped coin. If it
          lands on the table, it is a 1 (heads) or a 0 (tails). But if you
          spin the coin on the table, it is a blur of <em>both</em> heads
          and tails at the exact same time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How it helps:</strong> By keeping qubits in this fluid,
          spinning "superposition," a quantum computer can represent an
          exponentially large number of possible combinations of data at
          once, which quantum algorithms can then manipulate to solve
          certain problems more efficiently.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="concept">
      <h3>2. Entanglement (The Magic Twins)</h3>
      <p>
        In our everyday world, if you roll a die in New York and a friend
        rolls a die in Tokyo, your results have nothing to do with each
        other. Quantum mechanics allows a phenomenon called entanglement,
        where two quantum systems become deeply connected.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Rough Analogy:</strong> Imagine having two magic coins.
          You give one to a friend and take the other with you. No matter
          how far apart you travel, when you flip your coin and it lands on
          heads, your friend's coin will give you a correlated result,
          meaning you can know how the two outcomes are connected.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How it helps:</strong> Entanglement links qubits together
          in a way that means adding more qubits doesn't just add power
          linearly; it allows the computer to represent an exponentially
          larger number of possible states, creating a much larger space
          for quantum algorithms to work with.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="concept">
      <h3>3. Quantum Interference (The Rhythmic Waves)</h3>
      <p>
        Because quantum systems have wave-like properties, they can
        interact with each other in ways similar to ripples in a pond.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The Analogy:</strong> If you drop two rocks in water, the
          ripples will hit each other. In some places, the waves crash
          together and cancel each other out (destructive interference). In
          other places, they combine to make a bigger wave (constructive
          interference).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How it helps:</strong> A quantum computer uses
          interference to intentionally supress the incorrect paths to a
          problem and amplify the probability of a desired answer so it
          stands out clearly at the end of the calculation.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
      I'll attach some YouTube videos in the description that I watched to
      get a preliminary grasp of this. Again, it's not crucial to
      understand the nature of how these computers are built, but I wanted
      to highlight this because it clearly shows that we are stepping into
      a new realm of how we understand and use capabilities. Equipped with
      a rudimentary understanding of quantum computers, we can now move on
      to understanding why this capability boom has the fintech industry
      concerned.
    </p>

    <p>
      A large part of the security behind digital transactions,
      communications and networks relies on cryptography and cryptographic
      keys. A cryptography key is a unique piece of information, usually a
      very long string of random numbers and letters. It is used by a
      mathematical formula (a cipher) to scramble readable data into an
      unreadable code, or unscramble it back to normal. Much of what we do
      online is protected by cryptographic protocols that provide
      encryption, which is like a safe, and the specific key/combination to
      open is a cryptography key. Let me give you an example:
    </p>

    <p>
      When you and someone else want to send each other a secure message,
      the app automatically creates cryptographic keys for each of you.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        Your Friend's Public Key: (Can be shared openly)
        <p class="code-block">04a3b2c1...</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        Your Friend's Private Key: (Kept securely on their phone and never shared)
        <p class="code-block">9f8e7d6c...</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>Then you send him a message &mdash; "Are we heading out tonight?"</p>

    <p>
      So before the message leaves your phone, your messaging app uses your
      friend's Public Key, along with other cryptographic information
      exchanged between your devices, to establish a secure connection
      between the two of you.
    </p>

    <p>
      The readable text is then encrypted on your phone, turning it into a
      scrambled mess of random characters:
    </p>

    <p class="code-block">x8F!qZ9#mLp2@vR9W</p>

    <p>
      This scrambled code (x8F!qZ9#mLp2@vR9W) travels across the internet
      and passes through the computers that carry the message.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>If a hacker intercepts the data mid-transit, all they see is the gibberish.</li>
      <li>Even if someone intercepts the message as it travels across the network, they cannot read the contents without the necessary cryptographic keys.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The scrambled message arrives at your friend's phone. The
      cryptographic keys on their device are then used to decrypt the
      message and turn it back into:
    </p>

    <p class="code-block">"Are we heading out tonight?"</p>

    <p>
      This same broad family of cryptographic techniques upholds the
      security of everything from net banking and UPI to demat accounts. It
      is the backbone of how our cybersecurity infrastructure is built, and
      how we keep ourselves safe from hackers. But this very system that we
      have relied on for so many years is possibly going to be compromised
      when we do eventually reach quantum computing capabilities (which we
      still don't know when that will be). Because quantum computers could
      eventually solve the underlying mathematical problems behind today's
      encryption far more efficiently, they could potentially
      reverse-engineer the cryptographic protections we rely on and
      compromise many of the private keys securing our data. This threat is
      why it was a topic of discussion and various firms are seeing
      development of post-quantum cryptography to remain secure against
      attacks from sufficiently powerful quantum computers. While that is
      an ongoing exercise, what it means today is that we have a certain
      indication that our legacy security infrastructure is going to soon
      be dated. And the nature of the compromise is such that we cannot
      wait for quantum computers to be a reality before we make this
      change, it has to start today and start immediately, but how?
    </p>

    <p>
      That exact question was exactly what was being asked at the fest.
      What companies are trying to do now is move to being crypto agile.
      Crypto agile just means that companies will be able to build security
      systems so that cryptographic algorithms, keys and protocols can be
      quickly replaced or upgraded without rebuilding the entire
      infrastructure. It was the premise around both the talks I went to,
      and the conversations basically were that we needed to start
      designing and upgrading this infrastructure, because if we wait to do
      this when we eventually get quantum computers, that may be
      catastrophic.
    </p>

    <p>
      During one of the sessions there was a discussion on the magnitude of
      the task at hand, what it would require to make this transition to be
      quantum proof. The part of changing the cryptographic algorithms we
      rely on today to post-quantum alternatives such as ML-KEM (Kyber) and
      ML-DSA (Dilithium) is, I daresay, fairly straightforward. The
      algorithms/cryptographic systems have been studied, and the
      replacement of the network infrastructure, while tedious, is
      something that is straightforward. But the real mammoth task is
      replacing our physical legacy infrastructure all around India. It's
      simple, our bank cards rely on cryptographic systems such as RSA and
      ECC for certain security functions, and these will eventually need to
      move toward post-quantum alternatives. Like I said, I think while the
      algorithm transition is tedious and time consuming, it's still fairly
      straightforward. Now if you take one of these bank cards and go to
      withdraw cash from an ATM, the card and the terminal use these
      cryptographic protocols to authenticate the card and secure the
      transaction, before the bank's systems authorise the withdrawal and
      give you access to your money. But this would mean that the ATM is
      also required to have moved from the old keys to the new ones, which
      is where I believe the real mammoth task lies. Legacy infrastructure
      all over Patna, Pimpri-Chinchwad, Vellore would have to also make this
      transition. This is just one example, but because everything relies
      on this system for security we need to start taking our first steps
      to protecting ourselves.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

